============================================================OCI Generative AI============================================================
The OCI Generative AI dashboard provides several tools for working with foundation and custom models.

You can access the service from the OCI Console through Analytics & AI → AI Services → Generative AI.

The dashboard includes documentation, a service tour, a playground, dedicated AI clusters, custom models, and endpoints.

The Playground is a visual interface that allows you to experiment with hosted pretrained or custom models without writing code. 
You can test different use cases, refine your prompts and available model parameters, and see the generated responses. 
Once you are satisfied with the result, you can view the generated code and use it as a starting point for integrating Generative AI into your application.

A Dedicated AI Cluster provides GPU-based compute resources for workloads such as fine-tuning and hosting fine-tuned models.

A Custom Model is a model created by fine-tuning a pretrained foundation model with your own dataset.

An Endpoint is the access point through which an application can send inference requests to a hosted model.

So the concepts are:

Playground → experiment without code
Dedicated AI Cluster → GPU compute resources
Fine-tuning → customize a pretrained model
Custom Model → the resulting fine-tuned model
Endpoint → access the hosted model for inference

And if the dashboard shows no custom models, endpoints, or dedicated clusters, that can simply mean that no fine-tuning or dedicated cluster has been created yet.

*******************Embeddings
Embeddings are numerical representations of text. An embedding model takes a piece of text and converts it into a vector of numbers. 
For example, an embedding might contain hundreds of dimensions, such as 384 dimensions. 
Each dimension is just a numerical value, and the complete vector represents information about the meaning of the text.

The main reason we use embeddings is to make semantic search possible. Semantic search focuses on the meaning of the text rather than only looking for matching words.

This is different from lexical search. Lexical search mainly looks for the same words or terms. 
For example, a search for “technical skills” may look for documents containing those exact words. 
Semantic search can understand that “technical skills,” “professional abilities,” and “developing expertise” may have similar meanings, even though the words are different.

The HR Help Center example contains many articles. Each article is converted into an embedding. 
The embeddings can then be compared numerically. When embeddings are close to each other in the vector space, the corresponding texts are usually semantically similar.

The demonstration shows these embeddings as points on a two-dimensional graph. The real embeddings may have hundreds of dimensions, 
but a two-dimensional graph is easier for humans to visualize. The visualization shows that articles about technical skills, 
career development, and business skills can appear close together because their meanings are related. 
Other articles about vacation, leave, or time cards may form another group.

These groups are called clusters. A cluster is a group of data points whose embeddings are similar or close to each other.

Now imagine that a user asks:

“How can I improve my skills?”

The question is also converted into an embedding. 
The system compares the query embedding with the embeddings of the HR articles. 
It finds the articles whose vectors are closest to the query vector and returns them as relevant results.

So the overall process is:

Text
 ↓
Embedding Model
 ↓
Vector of Numbers
 ↓
Vector Similarity
 ↓
Semantic Search
 ↓
Relevant Documents

This concept is also very important for RAG. In a RAG system, documents are converted into embeddings and stored in a vector database.
When a user asks a question, the question is converted into an embedding, and semantic search finds the most relevant documents. 
Those documents are then provided to the LLM so it can generate a grounded answer.

So remember the complete connection:

Documents
   ↓
Embeddings
   ↓
Vector Database
   ↓
Semantic Search
   ↓
Relevant Documents
   ↓
LLM
   ↓
Answer

The key idea is:
Embeddings represent meaning as numbers, and semantic search uses the similarity between those numerical representations to find text with similar meaning.



============================================================VECTOR Search============================================================
AI Vector Search is built into Oracle Database 23ai. It allows the database to work with vector embeddings and perform similarity searches based on 
the semantic meaning of data.

A vector embedding is a numerical representation of text, images, or other data. For example, an image can be passed through an embedding model,
which produces a vector such as:

[0.21, -0.45, 0.73, 0.18, ...]

Oracle Database 23ai provides a VECTOR datatype for storing these embeddings directly in database tables.

Oracle also provides SQL support for generating vectors and performing similarity searches. This means that the database can become
an important part of a modern GenAI or RAG pipeline.

Oracle Database 23ai is also a converged database. Instead of being designed only for one type of data, it supports multiple data 
types and workloads, including relational data, JSON, XML, graph, spatial data, text, and vectors. This is useful because enterprise 
GenAI applications often need to work with different types of data at the same time.

Oracle provides the VECTOR_EMBEDDING function to generate an embedding using an embedding model. The model can be accessed through an API, 
or a model can be loaded into the database. In the example from the lesson, a ResNet-50 model is loaded into the database and used with an image.

The process is:

Query Image
     ↓
ResNet-50 Model
     ↓
VECTOR_EMBEDDING
     ↓
Query Vector

The database may already contain embeddings for many other images:

Image A → Vector A
Image B → Vector B
Image C → Vector C
Image D → Vector D

The query image is converted into its own vector, and AI Vector Search compares that vector with the stored vectors. 
The database can then return the images whose vectors are most similar to the query vector.

So the idea is:

Query Vector
     ↓
Similarity Search
     ↓
Compare with stored vectors
     ↓
Find closest vectors
     ↓
Return top matching results

Oracle also provides approximate vector search indexes that are designed to make similarity searches efficient, 
especially when the database contains a very large number of vectors. Instead of performing an expensive comparison against every vector,
approximate nearest-neighbor techniques can find very close matches much more efficiently.

Another important feature is the VECTOR datatype. A table can contain normal relational columns together with a vector column:

ID | Text | Embedding

The vector dimension can be specified, or a simpler VECTOR specification can be used. This provides flexibility as embedding models evolve
and new models with different vector dimensions become available.

The overall concept is:

Data
 ↓
Embedding Model
 ↓
VECTOR_EMBEDDING
 ↓
Vector
 ↓
VECTOR column
 ↓
Oracle AI Vector Search
 ↓
Similarity Search
 ↓
Relevant Results

And this connects directly to RAG:

Documents
     ↓
Embeddings
     ↓
Oracle VECTOR columns
     ↓
AI Vector Search
     ↓
Relevant Documents
     ↓
LLM
     ↓
Grounded Answer

So the key idea is that Oracle Database 23ai can store vectors, 
generate vectors, and search vectors based on similarity, while keeping those vectors alongside the organization's other relational and enterprise data.

****************************************************************************************************************************************************

The next step after storing vector embeddings is to search for similar vectors. 
Oracle provides the VECTOR_DISTANCE function to calculate the distance between two vectors. 
The important idea is that a smaller distance means that the vectors are more similar.

Smaller Distance → More Similar
Larger Distance  → Less Similar

Oracle uses COSINE as the default distance metric in this lesson, but the distance metric can be changed. 
The appropriate metric should be selected based on the model that generated the embeddings.

Once the database contains vector embeddings, we can use Vector Search directly in SQL. 
For example, we could compare a candidate's resume embedding with job-position embeddings and return the top ten positions 
that are semantically similar to the resume while also filtering the results by the candidate's preferred cities.

This is important because the query can combine normal relational data with AI Vector Search. The application does not need to perform these operations separately.

Oracle also provides Vector Indexes to make similarity searches more efficient. 
A Vector Index is created similarly to other database indexes, but it is designed for vector similarity search. 
Vector indexes are important not only for performance, but also because they can control the quality of approximate search results.

Oracle provides different index organization approaches. If the index can fit in memory, it can use an In-Memory Neighbor Graph. 
If it does not fit in memory, Neighbor Partitions can be used.

For approximate similarity search, we can use the APPROXIMATE keyword. Approximate search uses a vector index to find very similar results efficiently 
instead of performing a complete exact search over all vectors.

The TARGET ACCURACY setting controls the desired accuracy of approximate search results. 
For example, if the target accuracy is 80% and we request the top five results, the course uses the example of approximately 
four out of five results being correct compared with the exact results.

However, Oracle does not have to use the vector index for every query. If there is no suitable vector index, 
an exact search can be used. Also, the Cost-Based Optimizer can decide that using the index would be too expensive and choose another execution plan.

One important capability of Oracle Database 23ai is combining Vector Search with SQL JOINs. Enterprise data is usually normalized and 
stored across multiple tables. For example, we might have Author, Books, and Pages tables.

Each page can contain its own text embedding. We can take a user's text query, convert it into an embedding called qVec, 
and search the page embeddings for similar text. At the same time, we can join the tables and apply normal SQL conditions, 
such as selecting only fiction books whose authors are from India.

The query can therefore combine:

Vector Similarity
      +
SQL JOINs
      +
SQL Filters
      +
Top-K Results

This is very useful for enterprise applications because the data required for a useful answer is often distributed across multiple relational tables.

AI Vector Search can also be part of a complete GenAI pipeline. The original data can come from many sources, such as databases, CSV files, documents, or other data sources.

The data can first be loaded using a document loader. It can then be transformed, summarized, or split into smaller chunks. 
Each chunk can be converted into an embedding using an embedding model, and those embeddings can be stored in the vector database.

The overall process is:

Data Sources
     ↓
Document Loader
     ↓
Transform / Split into Chunks
     ↓
Embedding Model
     ↓
Vector Embeddings
     ↓
Oracle Database
     ↓
Vector Search
     ↓
Relevant Information

When a user asks a question, the question can also be converted into an embedding. Vector Search retrieves the most relevant pieces of information. 
This retrieval step can then be combined with an LLM using the RAG pattern.

User Query
     ↓
Query Embedding
     ↓
Vector Search
     ↓
Relevant Documents
     ↓
LLM
     ↓
Grounded Answer

Oracle also provides integration with third-party frameworks such as LangChain and LlamaIndex, allowing developers to build more sophisticated 
GenAI applications while using Oracle Database 23ai as part of the data and vector layer.

The main advantage of Oracle's approach is that AI Vector Search is integrated directly into the database. 
A single SQL-based solution can combine relational data, JSON, text, spatial data, graph data, machine learning capabilities, and vector similarity search.

So the main idea of this lesson is:

Oracle Database 23ai can perform vector similarity search directly on enterprise data, combine that search with 
normal SQL operations such as JOINs and filters, and use the results as part of a complete GenAI or RAG pipeline.


============================================================select AI ============================================================
Select AI → Natural Language to SQL
AI Profile → configuration for LLM/provider + database objects
LLM → generates SQL based on question + database context
Database Metadata → helps LLM understand schemas/tables/views
SHOWSQL → shows generated SQL
APEX → application/UI layer
OCI Generative AI → can provide LLM capabilities
Pluggability → ability to choose different model/provider
Natural Language Query → ask questions without knowing SQL/schema
Security → enterprise data protection within OCI tenancy when using the described OCI setup



Select AI is a capability in Oracle Autonomous Database that allows users to ask questions about their 
business data using natural language instead of writing SQL manually.

Normally, to query a database, you need to understand the database schema, table names, column names, 
relationships, joins, filters, and SQL syntax. With Select AI, the user can simply ask a question such as:

“What are the top 10 streamed movies?”

The overall process is:

Natural Language Question
          ↓
       Select AI
          ↓
          LLM
          ↓
     Generated SQL
          ↓
   Oracle Database
          ↓
      Result Set

The important point is that the LLM does not replace the database. The LLM helps generate the SQL, 
and the Oracle Database executes that SQL and returns the results.

Select AI uses an AI Profile to make this process work. The AI Profile defines important configuration such as 
which LLM provider and model should be used and which database schemas, tables, or views should participate in the process.

This is important because a general LLM does not automatically know an organization's private database. 
The model needs information about the relevant database objects so that it can understand the user's question and generate an appropriate SQL statement.

The process can be viewed as:

User Question
      +
Database Metadata
      +
AI Profile / Model Configuration
      ↓
Optimized Prompt
      ↓
LLM
      ↓
Generated SQL
      ↓
Oracle Database
      ↓
Result Set

Select AI can also show the SQL that it generated. This is done using SHOWSQL. 
This allows developers or database professionals to review the generated query and verify that it uses the correct tables, joins, filters, and logic.

For example:

User:
"Find the top customer segments for George Clooney movies."
              ↓
Select AI
              ↓
Generated SQL
              ↓
Oracle Database
              ↓
Result

This is especially useful because AI-generated SQL should be reviewed when accuracy and business logic are important.

Select AI can also be integrated into applications built with Oracle APEX. In an APEX application,
a user can enter a natural-language question, Select AI can generate the SQL, 
Oracle can execute it, and APEX can display the results using reports, filters, charts, or other application features.

Another important concept is pluggability. Select AI is not permanently tied to one LLM provider. 
Depending on the configuration and use case, different providers and models can be used, 
such as OCI Generative AI, Cohere, OpenAI, or Llama-based models. This also allows organizations to use newer or fine-tuned models as their requirements evolve.

Security is also important for enterprise use. With Autonomous Select AI and OCI Generative AI in the described OCI setup, 
enterprise data can remain within the organization's OCI tenancy rather than being sent to an unrelated external LLM provider or exposed to other customers.

The key difference between Select AI and AI Vector Search is:

Select AI:
Natural Language
      ↓
LLM
      ↓
SQL
      ↓
Database Result


AI Vector Search:
Data
 ↓
Embeddings
 ↓
Vector Search
 ↓
Similar / Relevant Data

So the main idea of this lesson is:

Select AI provides a natural-language interface to Oracle Database. It uses an LLM to understand the user's intent and generate SQL,
while Oracle Database remains responsible for executing the SQL and returning the actual business data.

And the most important exam sentence is:

Select AI translates natural-language questions into SQL using an LLM and database metadata,
allowing users to query Oracle Database without needing to know the underlying schema or SQL syntax.

Select AI enhancement:It allows users to interact with Autonomous Database using natural language instead of having to write SQL or understand the database schema.

